@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title PLAN — model resolution: operator intent to harness id, in two phases

component "operator intent\nARCHITECT_MODEL=kimi-3\nEDITOR_MODEL=grok-4.5\nSMALL_MODEL=grok-4.5-fast" as INTENT <<app>>

node "PHASE 1 — host, BEFORE the container exists" as P1 <<host>> {
  component "normalize\nlowercase · separators · last segment\ndated suffix · vendor prefix" as NORM <<app>>
  component "catalog lookup\ncanonical id + provider" as LOOK <<app>>
  component "provider union\nof the three roles" as UNION <<app>>
  component "broker routes\n+ Squid ACL" as WIRE <<app>>
}

node "PHASE 2 — container entrypoint" as P2 <<host>> {
  component "translate\ncanonical -> this harness's id" as XLAT <<app>>
  component "verify (where listable)\nagainst the harness registry" as VERIFY <<app>>
  component "export role vars\nCECLI_* · OPENCODE_* · ANTHROPIC_*" as EXPORT <<app>>
}

database "agent-settings.yml\ntargets.<t>.models\nmain · editor · small\nCANONICAL ids" as CACHE <<db>>

INTENT ARROW_MAIN NORM
NORM ARROW_MAIN LOOK
LOOK ARROW_MAIN UNION
UNION ARROW_MAIN WIRE
LOOK ARROW_OPTIONAL CACHE : remember what resolved
CACHE ARROW_OPTIONAL NORM : default when intent is absent
LOOK ARROW_MAIN XLAT : canonical crosses the boundary
XLAT ARROW_OPTIONAL VERIFY
VERIFY ARROW_MAIN EXPORT

note right of P1
  **Why phase 1 cannot be skipped.** The provider set decides the broker
  route table and the Squid ACL, and both are argv on ""docker run"" — they
  must be settled before the container exists. In-container resolution is
  therefore impossible to rely on for anything security-bearing, no matter
  how authoritative it is.

  So phase 1 must be **offline and deterministic**: a checked-in table, no
  network, same answer every run.
end note

note right of P2
  **Why phase 2 cannot be skipped either.** Only the harness knows which
  ids its own build accepts. The catalog says what the vendor published;
  the installed version is the authority on what it will take.

  Verification is per-harness and only where a documented listing command
  exists — discovered by the sync skill, never assumed. Where none exists,
  the id is exported and the harness reports its own error, which is no
  worse than today.
end note

note as Miss #F4F9FB
  **A catalog miss must never block a run.** An id the table has not synced
  yet — a model released this week — passes through **verbatim with a
  warning**. Three reasons this is safe:

  * The route table injects every detected provider, so an unattributable
    model still authenticates as long as its key is present. Correctness
    lives in the routes, not the catalog.
  * A catalog that gates the operator makes proveo the reason a brand-new
    model cannot be used. Unacceptable for a table refreshed by hand.
  * The failure is legible: the harness names the id it rejected.

  So the catalog is an **ergonomics and warning layer**. This is what makes
  requirement 3 non-fatal, and it is the direct consequence of requirement 1.
end note

note bottom of NORM
  **Intent is loose; ids are exact.** ""kimi-3"", ""kimi k3"" and ""Kimi K3"" are
  the same intent. Normalization lowercases, collapses space/underscore/dot
  to a single separator, takes the last path segment (so
  ""openrouter/vendor/model"" reduces), and strips a dated suffix — the
  existing ""normalizeModelID"" already does the last three.

  Matching is then EXACT against canonical ids plus per-row aliases. Never
  fuzzy: two candidates means **list them and stop**, because guessing
  wrong spends the operator's money on the wrong model and reads as
  success.
end note

note bottom of CACHE
  **Canonical ids, never harness ids.** Storing the translated spelling
  would pin the cache to whatever the table said on the day it was written,
  so a synced correction would never reach it. Canonical survives the sync;
  the harness id is re-derived every run.

  Per target, because a model valid for opencode may be unsupported for
  cursor. Precedence is unchanged from the choice cache: **explicit env or
  flag > cached > unset**.

  **A remembered id is kept verbatim, not validated.**
  ""RolesFromCanonical"" copies every non-empty value through — no catalog
  lookup, so "no longer resolves" is not a state this path can observe, and
  nothing warns or drops. proveo cannot tell a catalog that is //behind//
  from a model that is //gone//, and the harness that owns the model list
  gives the better error than a launcher guessing from a table it may not
  have synced. What that preserves either way: proveo never hard-fails on a
  file it wrote itself. Drift record:
  ""_spec/_plans/role-cache-resolution-drift\.puml"".

  **The merge is per role, not per set.** A remembered role is applied only
  where the operator set nothing, so a run never silently contradicts an
  ""ARCHITECT_MODEL"" they exported themselves — one explicit role does not
  discard the two remembered ones beside it.

  Shown read-only in the prompt header beside the workspace and ""\.env"" —
  NOT as a form row. The radio grid stays radios; models are typed
  elsewhere.
end note
@enduml
